{"id":"rel.wave2.batch04.jacobson-ring.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.03b55b8ca41abb20.jacobson-ring","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"radical ideal","editorial_reason":"The Jacobson condition involves radical ideals and maximal ideals of a commutative ring; these are special kinds of ring ideals, with radicality and maximality still distinct prerequisites.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FZ","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_scope_comparison_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch04.pure-ideal-intersection-test.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.3fe40ac4fb74bff2.pure-ideal-intersection-test","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"ideal I","editorial_reason":"Purity by intersections and products quantifies over ordinary ring ideals, which are two-sided ideals in a commutative ring; the underlying ideal operation is required.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04PQ","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_scope_comparison_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
